In your `App.xaml.cs`, override `CreateWindow` and subscribe to the lifecycle events:

```csharp
protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
{
    var window = base.CreateWindow(activationState);

    window.Created  += (s, e) => Debug.WriteLine("Window Created");
    window.Activated   += (s, e) => Debug.WriteLine("Window Activated");
    window.Deactivated += (s, e) => Debug.WriteLine("Window Deactivated");
    window.Stopped     += (s, e) => Debug.WriteLine("Window Stopped");
    window.Resumed     += (s, e) => Debug.WriteLine("Window Resumed");
    window.Destroying  += (s, e) => Debug.WriteLine("Window Destroying");

    // Size/position changes
    window.SizeChanged += (s, e) => Debug.WriteLine($"SizeChanged: {window.Width}x{window.Height}");

    // Background/foreground (available on mobile)
    window.Backgrounding += (s, e) => Debug.WriteLine("Backgrounding");
    window.DisplayDensityChanged += (s, e) => Debug.WriteLine($"DisplayDensity: {e.DisplayDensity}");

    return window;
}
```

**Lifecycle order by platform:**

| Transition | Android | iOS/Mac | Windows |
|---|---|---|---|
| App launch | Created → Activated | Created → Activated | Created → Activated |
| Backgrounded | Deactivated → Stopped | Deactivated | Deactivated |
| Restored | Resumed → Activated | Activated | Activated |
| Closed | Destroying | Destroying | Destroying |

> **Tip:** Use `System.Diagnostics.Debug.WriteLine` so output appears in the IDE's Debug Output window without affecting release builds.
